**Read:** [Introduction to Mining Engineering — Penn State MNG 230](../sources/Penn-State-Introduction-to-Mining.lingua#r_5NAY6K47Y4Y61V4JF74JJNV4Q2), the complete introduction, commodities, and lifecycle sequence; [Hard Rock Miner’s Handbook — Jack de la Vergne, fifth edition](../sources/Hard-Rock-Miners-Handbook.lingua#r_18YBY5XX4TFS7ZQSKTQMW34EFA), the introduction and full overview sections; [Mineral Commodity Summaries 2026 — USGS](../../../../gaia/earth/geology/sources/Mineral-Commodity-Summaries-USGS.lingua#r_47FJ4GZC4CS4KJX8WXNDB418CX), the full relevant commodity chapter with definitions/data-year notes.

Mining and mineral engineering connect geological material with extraction, handling, concentration, utilities, people, and the long-lived changes left behind. Begin with a concrete question: what material is needed, where might it occur in usable form, and what work and evidence would connect that occurrence with a dependable product?

A geological occurrence, a reported resource, an extractable reserve, a tonne of run-of-mine material, and a delivered concentrate are different claims. Each involves a defined material, quantity, quality, context, and set of uncertainties. A high assay or an appealing diagram does not establish the whole production chain.

[Geology — Earth materials, processes, history, and investigation](../../../../gaia/earth/geology/Geology.lingua#r_3CK43VQS79NK0STWNPSCS8YM06) and [Mineralogy and petrology — minerals, crystals, rock processes, and investigation](../../../../gaia/earth/mineralogy-and-petrology/Mineralogy-and-petrology.lingua#r_44F7X6GPMW6ZSCCN6D71QSVKBN) explain material origins, structures, mineral hosts, texture, and observations. This route applies those shared foundations to mine systems and their interfaces. Quantitative, chemical, measurement, and computational preparation appears where used rather than becoming an unrelated degree prerequisite.

**Work:** choose an ordinary item or material and draw its upstream mineral connection using an identified commodity/reference source. Mark what is observed, estimated, assumed, or unavailable. Form two questions requiring different engineering evidence, such as product quality and excavation behavior, and explain why one analysis cannot answer both.

Read the full opening course sections and handbook introduction. Keep the original account as later planning, processing, water, operation, and closure studies expose dependencies that the first sketch missed.

**Continue:** [Describe a mine as connected physical, informational, and working systems](Mine-lifecycle-and-connected-production-systems.lingua#r_0WEGJD1A06ZDDW1CMPYCCZE9G6).

